WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:02.582
YURI GRINSHTEYN: Do you
run distributed workloads

00:00:02.582 --> 00:00:03.690
on Kubernetes or GKE?

00:00:03.690 --> 00:00:06.120
If so, you are probably
having to manage

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:09.330
more and more services, and
it's getting harder or harder

00:00:09.330 --> 00:00:12.310
to understand what's going
on if there are any issues.

00:00:12.310 --> 00:00:14.250
This is where a
service mesh can help.

00:00:14.250 --> 00:00:16.740
A service mesh is a dedicated
infrastructure layer

00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:18.660
that you can add
to your clusters.

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:21.390
It gives you a capabilities
like observability, traffic

00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:23.670
management, and
security, without having

00:00:23.670 --> 00:00:25.410
to modify application code.

00:00:25.410 --> 00:00:26.310
My name is Yuri.

00:00:26.310 --> 00:00:27.840
I'm an SRE at Google Cloud.

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:30.720
And today I'm going to show you
how to use both the open source

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:32.400
version of the
Istio service mesh

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:35.100
and Google's managed
Anthos Service Mesh

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:38.760
to get better observability for
your services running a GKE.

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:42.094
This is Engineering
for Reliability.

00:00:42.094 --> 00:00:44.354
[LIGHT MUSIC]

00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:48.530
The Istio service mesh works
by adding Envoy proxy sidecars

00:00:48.530 --> 00:00:50.480
to your services running on GKE.

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:53.240
These proxies intercept all
service-to-service traffic

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:56.030
in the mesh and enable the
application-aware features

00:00:56.030 --> 00:00:58.640
like security, traffic
management and routing,

00:00:58.640 --> 00:00:59.900
and observability.

00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:01.610
This is the data plane.

00:01:01.610 --> 00:01:04.190
The control plane dynamically
programs the proxy

00:01:04.190 --> 00:01:06.950
sidecars, updating them as
the rules or the environment

00:01:06.950 --> 00:01:07.940
change.

00:01:07.940 --> 00:01:10.610
Your first option for
using Istio in GKE

00:01:10.610 --> 00:01:12.320
is to simply install
the open source

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:13.862
distribution in your cluster.

00:01:13.862 --> 00:01:15.320
This is the way to
go if you prefer

00:01:15.320 --> 00:01:17.300
to have the most control
over your deployment

00:01:17.300 --> 00:01:19.790
or if you have existing
dependencies on features that

00:01:19.790 --> 00:01:23.060
may not yet be available in
Google's managed offering.

00:01:23.060 --> 00:01:25.700
Istio includes an open
source observability toolkit.

00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:28.550
You can use Prometheus for
metrics and Zipkin or Jaeger

00:01:28.550 --> 00:01:30.080
for analyzing tracing.

00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:32.150
You can also enable
proxy access logs

00:01:32.150 --> 00:01:34.460
and have the proxy
sidecars write out logs

00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:36.260
for every request in the mesh.

00:01:36.260 --> 00:01:38.540
Let's have a look at how
you can use this toolkit

00:01:38.540 --> 00:01:43.670
and centralize all that
telemetry to Cloud Operations.

00:01:43.670 --> 00:01:45.980
In our example, we have
an application running

00:01:45.980 --> 00:01:47.888
in the Istio demo namespace.

00:01:57.860 --> 00:02:00.800
We have the Istio control plane
and observability components

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:03.110
running in the Istio
system namespace.

00:02:08.419 --> 00:02:10.669
This includes Prometheus
for metrics and Kiali

00:02:10.669 --> 00:02:14.302
for observing
traffic in the mesh.

00:02:14.302 --> 00:02:16.260
If, for example, we want
to look at the request

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:19.800
rate for our
services in the mesh,

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:34.380
we can use the istioctl
dashboard prometheus command

00:02:34.380 --> 00:02:35.820
and run a query like this.

00:02:40.110 --> 00:02:41.790
If we want to
visualize how traffic

00:02:41.790 --> 00:02:43.950
flows between the different
services in the mesh,

00:02:43.950 --> 00:02:44.870
we can use Kiali.

00:02:57.270 --> 00:03:01.687
At each node we can see how much
traffic the service is seeing

00:03:01.687 --> 00:03:03.270
and what the response
time looks like.

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:12.480
Finally, we can enable request
logging for the Envoy side car

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:14.820
proxies by using
a telemetry API.

00:03:22.477 --> 00:03:24.310
We can then view those
logs in the terminal.

00:03:32.620 --> 00:03:35.050
Now let's have a look at how
we can get this telemetry

00:03:35.050 --> 00:03:38.790
into Cloud Operations.

00:03:38.790 --> 00:03:40.470
To adjust the
Prometheus metrics,

00:03:40.470 --> 00:03:54.420
we can actually use Google's
Managed Prometheus binary just

00:03:54.420 --> 00:03:55.770
by replacing this image tag.

00:03:59.020 --> 00:04:01.037
This allows us to query
Prometheus metrics

00:04:01.037 --> 00:04:01.870
in Metrics Explorer.

00:04:19.430 --> 00:04:20.990
Here's that same
request for a query.

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:28.200
The proxy sidecar request
logs are automatically

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:29.460
ingested into Cloud Logging.

00:04:36.340 --> 00:04:37.660
And we can query those, too.

00:04:43.810 --> 00:04:46.060
So through the combination
of Google's Managed Service

00:04:46.060 --> 00:04:48.160
for Prometheus
and Cloud Logging,

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:50.350
we can get metric and
log telemetry generated

00:04:50.350 --> 00:04:52.750
by Istio ingested
into Cloud Operations

00:04:52.750 --> 00:04:54.467
and use it just
like other telemetry

00:04:54.467 --> 00:04:56.050
from your infrastructure
and services.

00:04:58.600 --> 00:05:01.240
If you want to maximize control
over your Istio deployment

00:05:01.240 --> 00:05:03.790
and possibly have experience
with Istio already,

00:05:03.790 --> 00:05:05.440
using the open
source distribution

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:06.890
is the right way to go.

00:05:06.890 --> 00:05:08.980
However, if you're new
to using a service mesh

00:05:08.980 --> 00:05:11.620
or would simply like a
more managed experience,

00:05:11.620 --> 00:05:14.200
there's the Anthos
Service Mesh or ASM.

00:05:14.200 --> 00:05:16.810
It's a tested and supported
distribution of Istio,

00:05:16.810 --> 00:05:18.820
giving you all the
benefits of a service mesh

00:05:18.820 --> 00:05:20.680
with full Google support.

00:05:20.680 --> 00:05:22.570
If you'd like to
control version upgrades

00:05:22.570 --> 00:05:25.540
yourself or have existing
integrations you depend on,

00:05:25.540 --> 00:05:28.720
you can deploy ASM with an
in-cluster control plane.

00:05:28.720 --> 00:05:30.340
Let's have a look
at such a deployment

00:05:30.340 --> 00:05:35.525
and how it automatically sends
telemetry to Cloud Operations.

00:05:35.525 --> 00:05:37.150
Let's first have a
look at our cluster.

00:05:41.500 --> 00:05:44.110
The control plane pods are
running in the Istio system

00:05:44.110 --> 00:05:47.020
namespace, just like what we had
when we installed open source

00:05:47.020 --> 00:05:47.520
Istio.

00:05:54.820 --> 00:05:57.460
Our application pods are also
instrumented with the Istio

00:05:57.460 --> 00:05:59.927
proxy containers.

00:05:59.927 --> 00:06:01.510
Now let's have a
look at the telemetry

00:06:01.510 --> 00:06:03.093
that the Anthos
Service Mesh collects.

00:06:16.630 --> 00:06:19.098
The Anthos Service Mesh home
page in the Cloud Console

00:06:19.098 --> 00:06:20.890
has a list of the
services that are running

00:06:20.890 --> 00:06:22.015
in the mesh in our cluster.

00:06:26.540 --> 00:06:28.900
We can select a service and
get some basic information

00:06:28.900 --> 00:06:35.490
about it, like request
rates, error rate,

00:06:35.490 --> 00:06:37.780
and average latency.

00:06:37.780 --> 00:06:40.110
The topology view here
shows us how the service

00:06:40.110 --> 00:06:41.490
sends and receives traffic.

00:06:45.720 --> 00:06:47.820
The Metrics screen allows
us to see these metrics

00:06:47.820 --> 00:06:50.758
over time, broken down
by various dimensions.

00:06:53.900 --> 00:06:56.060
Finally, we can get error
logs for our service

00:06:56.060 --> 00:06:57.740
by going to the
Diagnostics screen.

00:07:00.980 --> 00:07:02.690
We can click out to
the Logs Explorer

00:07:02.690 --> 00:07:04.580
to get more details
about these error logs.

00:07:20.230 --> 00:07:22.222
This is also where we
can view the access logs

00:07:22.222 --> 00:07:22.930
for our services.

00:07:31.600 --> 00:07:34.254
We can get more metrics
telemetry in Cloud Monitoring.

00:07:43.830 --> 00:07:48.690
example, we can run
a query like this

00:07:48.690 --> 00:07:50.685
to get a breakdown of
request rates by service.

00:07:54.130 --> 00:07:56.650
So this is how you can use
telemetry automatically

00:07:56.650 --> 00:07:59.112
ingested from the
in-cluster ASM control plane

00:07:59.112 --> 00:08:01.570
to understand the behavior and
the health of your services.

00:08:04.280 --> 00:08:05.900
If you're just
starting out with Istio

00:08:05.900 --> 00:08:08.000
or would like to use a
fully managed service,

00:08:08.000 --> 00:08:09.680
you can use Managed ASM.

00:08:09.680 --> 00:08:11.930
In this deployment
model, the control plane

00:08:11.930 --> 00:08:14.990
is fully managed by Google and
does not run in your cluster.

00:08:14.990 --> 00:08:17.630
It does mean that some features,
like custom metric adapters,

00:08:17.630 --> 00:08:18.470
won't work.

00:08:18.470 --> 00:08:20.780
If you need advanced
features like these,

00:08:20.780 --> 00:08:23.610
the open source distribution
may be the way to go for you.

00:08:23.610 --> 00:08:26.750
If not, let's have a look
at a managed ASM deployment

00:08:26.750 --> 00:08:28.670
and how its telemetry
is automatically

00:08:28.670 --> 00:08:32.860
ingested into Cloud Operations.

00:08:32.860 --> 00:08:35.230
Let's again start by
looking at our cluster.

00:08:35.230 --> 00:08:37.960
In this case, we don't actually
have any Istio control plane

00:08:37.960 --> 00:08:41.020
pods running in the
Istio system namespace.

00:08:41.020 --> 00:08:43.299
The control plane is
entirely managed by Google,

00:08:43.299 --> 00:08:45.070
and there are no
control plane components

00:08:45.070 --> 00:08:46.840
running in the cluster itself.

00:08:46.840 --> 00:08:49.060
Our application paths,
however, are still

00:08:49.060 --> 00:08:52.120
instrumented with the Istio
proxy sidecar container.

00:08:52.120 --> 00:08:54.490
Now let's have a look at
the collected telemetry.

00:09:04.580 --> 00:09:06.697
Just like before, we
can look at the services

00:09:06.697 --> 00:09:07.655
running in our cluster.

00:09:12.580 --> 00:09:14.830
For all of them, we can get
the same basic telemetry,

00:09:14.830 --> 00:09:16.045
including the topology view.

00:09:21.660 --> 00:09:37.640
We can get the same
metric telemetry,

00:09:37.640 --> 00:09:39.800
and we can get the
same access logs.

00:09:39.800 --> 00:09:42.200
All of this is made available
via the managed control

00:09:42.200 --> 00:09:44.300
plane without the
need to have anything

00:09:44.300 --> 00:09:47.120
running in the cluster itself.

00:09:47.120 --> 00:09:48.583
Thanks so much for
watching today.

00:09:48.583 --> 00:09:50.750
We learned how a service
mesh improves observability

00:09:50.750 --> 00:09:52.670
for distributed
workloads run in GKE

00:09:52.670 --> 00:09:55.640
and how you can use Cloud
Operations to get telemetry

00:09:55.640 --> 00:09:58.460
from both open source Istio
and the Anthos Service Mesh

00:09:58.460 --> 00:10:00.290
to observe your own services.

00:10:00.290 --> 00:10:02.660
I hope this helps you to keep
your applications reliable

00:10:02.660 --> 00:10:04.040
and your users happy.

00:10:04.040 --> 00:10:06.320
Don't forget to like and
subscribe to never miss out

00:10:06.320 --> 00:10:09.110
on more "Engineering for
Reliability with Google Cloud."

00:10:09.110 --> 00:10:10.670
See you soon.

00:10:10.670 --> 00:10:13.420
[UPBEAT MUSIC]

